{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"All four verified. Answers below.\n\n## 1. You're right, my framing was sloppy\n\n\"Disposable scaffolding\" described the harness's `.claude/worktrees/` pattern and I over-generalized it to worktrees as such. A worktree is just a checkout of a branch into its own folder — your worktree-per-PR usage is completely ordinary.\n\nBut the argument survives the correction, and your usage actually makes the current design fit *better*, not worse. If worktree ≈ branch ≈ PR in your workflow, then branch reachability already gives you exactly worktree-grained scoping — while surviving `git worktree remove` after the PR merges. Per-worktree storage would give you the same scoping *and* delete the conversations when you clean up the folder. The branch is the unit with a lifecycle worth attaching records to; the folder is a view onto it. That's the claim I should have made instead of the one about disposability.\n\n## 2. Distribution vs. visibility — you've found the sharper problem\n\nAgreed, and your reason is the one that matters: reachability filtering is a nicety in `cledger log`, but `cledger export` defaults to **everything**, no reachability filter at all. So an automated consumer like intent-recall gets abandoned-branch conversations by default, and a teammate who fetches gets them on disk regardless. Visibility is a read-layer convenience; distribution is wholesale. Two different scopes wearing one name.\n\nOptions, with a recommendation:\n\n- **Scoped push** (push only events reachable from the branch being pushed) is the real fix but fights the storage model. The notes ref is one ref — you can't partially push it, you'd have to synthesize a filtered notes commit per push, and it works against the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge that makes concurrent branches conflict-free. Real, but a project of its own.\n- **Scope the consumer contract instead**: `readEvents`/`export` already accept `reachableFrom`; today it's opt-in and undocumented as the thing automated consumers should pass.\n\nMy recommendation is to stop papering over it: state plainly in the docs that **the ledger's distribution unit is the repo, and reachability scopes reading only** — then make `export` reachability-aware by default with an explicit `--all` escape, so automated consumers get the scoped view unless they ask for everything. That inverts the current default, which is the wrong way round for exactly the consumers you're describing. Scoped push becomes a roadmap entry rather than a pretence. Say the word and I'll do it.\n\n## 3. Per-worktree storage: no new mechanism, but it doesn't buy what you'd want\n\nNot an entirely different mechanism — git already provides `refs/worktree/*`, which I verified is genuinely per-worktree. But it gives you **pointer isolation, not storage isolation**: worktrees share one object database (`.git/objects` lives in the common dir), so the note content would still physically sit in the primary clone. You'd have a per-worktree ref pointing at objects everyone shares.\n\nAnd it fails on the two things you'd actually need: those refs are destroyed with the worktree, and they don't push — you'd have to map them onto a normal ref for transport, which puts you right back in a shared namespace. So the isolation would be cosmetic while the durability loss would be real.\n\n## 4. The split you want already exists — but there's a live footgun next to it\n\n`redact.knownSecrets` is a **boolean toggle only**. The actual plaintext lives in `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, inside `.git`, structurally impossible to track. So a committed `.cledger.json` carries settings and never values. Your instinct was right and the design already honors it.\n\nThe one place a user could still footgun themselves is `redact.patterns` — those are meant to be *shapes*, but nothing stops someone committing a pattern with a literal credential in it.\n\nMore importantly, I found a real fail-open bug while checking this, and it bites you specifically. Config merges **per top-level section, not per key**: `const redact = override.redact ?? base.redact`. Your global config is exactly `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. So the day any repo commits a `.cledger.json` containing *any* `redact` key — say just a `patterns` list — the whole `redact` section is replaced, `knownSecrets` silently reverts to `false`, and known-secret scrubbing stops in that repo with no warning. Same shape as the worktree bug: silent, fails open, and only visible if you go looking.\n\nI'd fix that by deep-merging within `redact` (or at minimum warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config had set). Want me to take that plus the export-default change together?\n\nresult: Answered all four — conceded the worktree/branch framing error while showing branch-reachability still fits your worktree-per-PR workflow better than per-worktree storage would; confirmed `refs/worktree/*` gives only pointer isolation over a shared object store; confirmed `.cledger.json` holds the knownSecrets *toggle* while plaintext stays inside `.git`. Two real issues surfaced and awaiting your go-ahead: `export` defaults to unscoped so automated consumers see abandoned-branch data, and config merges per-section so a committed `.cledger.json` with any `redact` key silently disables your global `knownSecrets`.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"45cdb9d8b6522ff9e2f460fef45e48b1ac91340f7a889a818eb97826463d89b7","head":"47e117b2e20969745be383f2ab62bcc21a3dc5a5","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":847},"id":"ev1-07cfadb9bcbab13d6de13ba2a2785d4457c0be03c29762aa80ac9bcf7ac2014e","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-28T02:44:53.888Z","producer":{"model":"cl