{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both done. `main` is at `47e117b`, pushed, ledger synced, no branches or worktrees left.\n\n## Your notes question — you were right to push back on my framing\n\nThe notes ref is **shared, not per-worktree**. There is exactly one `refs/notes/conversation-ledger` for the whole repo, living in the common dir, and every worktree writes to it. I confirmed this with a controlled experiment (external worktree, capture from it, inspect refs): no per-worktree notes ref is ever created.\n\nSo neither of your two guesses is quite it. It's not that this conversation landed in the core folder because it *started* there — while I was in the worktree, capture anchored to the worktree's commit `3af216f`, which is in the shared notes ref.\n\nWhat's worktree-scoped isn't storage, it's **visibility**. Events anchor to HEAD at capture time, and `cledger log` filters by reachability from your current branch. So for a Claude Code window opened inside a worktree, whether at `.claude/worktrees/` or anywhere else:\n\n- Conversations record into the **main project's** notes ref immediately — nothing is stored in the worktree.\n- They're anchored to the worktree branch's commits, so `cledger log` from main **won't show them** until you merge. `cledger log --all` shows them right away.\n- On merge they \"carry over\" in the sense that they become reachable. No data moves, because it was never separate.\n- If you delete that branch unmerged, the events survive in the notes ref but sit on unreachable commits — visible only via `--all`. That's what `cledger re-anchor` exists for.\n\n## What I found while checking, and fixed\n\nChasing your question turned up something worse than what I'd reported. Because cursors were per-worktree, entering and leaving a worktree restarted the transcript scan — and append-time dedup only consults the note of the commit it's writing to, so the re-scan filed a *second copy* of the same events under the new HEAD. **351 duplicated event ids in your ledger**, and `cledger log --all` was returning 3423 events where only 3072 were real.\n\nSo 0.13.0 covers both halves:\n\n- **`commonDir`** via `--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir` (with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31), with all state and the hook path hanging off it. Git draws no distinction between a nested and an arbitrary-path worktree, so one fix covers both — and the tests deliberately use an *external* worktree, the case less likely to be exercised by accident.\n- **Read-side dedup**, because the ledger is append-only and those 351 duplicates can't be deleted — only read through. Earliest `recorded_at` wins, canonical serialization breaks ties so every clone picks the same copy. This one isn't worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves reproduces it.\n\nI left `.cledger.json` per-worktree on purpose — it's a tracked working-tree file, so being absent from a worktree until committed is correct for versioned repo config, unlike `.git`-side state.\n\nVerified against your real repo rather than only fixtures: scan from a worktree went 213 findings → 0, `log --all` went 3423/3072 → 3072/3072, and 150 tests pass. I also checked the new tests fail without the fix — 5 of 6 do, the sixth being the dedup test, which is independent by design.\n\nOne caveat: an allowlist entry or known secret you added *from* a worktree before this fix is orphaned in that worktree's private dir and won't migrate. Re-run `cledger allow` or `cledger redact --pattern` if something reappears.\n\nresult: cledger 0.13.0 on main (47e117b) — local state and the pre-push hook now resolve to the shared git dir for both nested and arbitrary-path worktrees, plus read-side dedup that collapsed 351 duplicate events in the real ledger; confirmed worktree conversations always record to the main project's shared notes ref and become visible on main at merge.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"c8094453047f0e5f8e3169519803050cbb5af009984b2352d836b00fd1c0cbbb","head":"47e117b2e20969745be383f2ab62bcc21a3dc5a5","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":788},"id":"ev1-456ef3c9e59d0145a6619beaa8ca2782dc87d6e826547d6b381a4d4a3413d705","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-27T07:36:30.561Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.13.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Both done. `main` is at `47e117b`, pushed, ledger synced, no branches or worktrees left.\n\n## Your notes question — you were right to push back on my framing\n\nThe notes ref is **shared, not per-worktree**. There is exactly one `refs/notes/conversation-ledger` for the whole repo, living in the common dir, and every worktree writes to it. I confirmed this with a controlled experiment (external worktree, capture from it, inspect refs): no per-worktree notes ref is ever created.\n\nSo neither of your two guesses is quite it. It's not that this conversation landed in the core folder because it *started* there — while I was in the worktree, capture anchored to the worktree's commit `3af216f`, which is in the shared notes ref.\n\nWhat's worktree-scoped isn't storage, it's **visibility**. Events anchor to HEAD at capture time, and `cledger log` filters by reachability from your current branch. So for a Claude Code window opened inside a worktree, whether at `.claude/worktrees/` or anywhere else:\n\n- Conversations record into the **main project's** notes ref immediately — nothing is stored in the worktree.\n- They're anchored to the w