{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Four answers.\n\n## 1. The sticky-note comparison — worth taking seriously, but the failure mode differs\n\nI should be straight with you on one thing first: **a per-worktree notes ref is technically possible.** Git has a `refs/worktree/*` namespace that is genuinely per-worktree — I just verified it: a ref created there from a worktree is invisible from the main checkout. So storing conversations per-worktree wasn't ruled out by git; it was a design choice.\n\nIt's the right one, though, and the concrete reason is lifecycle: **`refs/worktree/*` refs are destroyed with the worktree.** `git worktree remove` would take the conversations with it — precisely the loss the ledger exists to prevent. Worktrees are disposable scaffolding; branches are the durable unit, and branches are what merge. Reachability scopes to the branch, which is the thing that actually has a lifecycle worth tracking.\n\nSo the boundary here is the repo — the same boundary the code already has — and nothing crosses it that the code doesn't. That's different in kind from syncing everything to a cloud with no boundary at all.\n\nBut your instinct isn't wrong, and here's the sharpest true version of it: **the notes ref pushes wholesale.** A ref push sends the entire ref, so events from a branch you abandoned and never merged still go to origin and reach collaborators, even though `cledger log` will never show them to anyone. Reachability scopes *visibility*, not *distribution*. That's a real gap between what you'd expect and what happens, it's adjacent to the post-push purge roadmap item, and it's currently undocumented. Worth its own roadmap entry if you want me to write one up.\n\n## 2. Reachability\n\nNoted — and to be explicit about why it should help: the scoping you wanted is real, it's just implemented at the read layer rather than the storage layer.\n\n## 3. What `--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir` evaluates to\n\nYour primary clone's **`.git` directory** — not its working folder. Measured:\n\n| run from | `--absolute-git-dir` | `--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir` | `--show-toplevel` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| main checkout `…/r` | `…/r/.git` | `…/r/.git` | `…/r` |\n| worktree `…/w` | `…/r/.git/worktrees/w` | `…/r/.git` | `…/w` |\n\nTwo things to note. The common dir is constant across every working tree — that's the whole point. And `--path-format=absolute` is doing real work: without it, `--git-common-dir` prints a bare relative `.git` from the main checkout while already being absolute from a worktree, so the result needs resolving either way. That inconsistency is why the fallback path for git < 2.31 resolves by hand.\n\n## 4. `.cledger.json` — you're right, and I was sloppy\n\nYou have none. It doesn't exist in this repo and isn't tracked anywhere in it — I checked. It's an **optional** per-repo config file that cledger reads if present and ignores if absent; it never creates one.\n\nConfig resolves in two layers: `~/.config/cledger/config.json` at the user level (this is where your global `redact.knownSecrets` toggle actually lives), with `.cledger.json` in the repo root overriding it per key — `enabled`, `redact`, `scan`, `transport`, `reanchor`. The `{\"enabled\": false}` per-repo kill switch from 0.11.0 is the main reason to create one.\n\nMy phrasing was wrong: I called it \"a tracked working-tree file\" when it's an *optional file in the working tree, tracked only if you choose to commit it*. The narrow point I was making was just that it lives on the checkout side rather than the `.git` side, so unlike the state I moved to `commonDir`, a worktree only sees it if it's been committed — which is correct behavior for versioned config, not something to fix. That's also what made my first version of that test fail.\n\nresult: Answered all four — per-worktree notes were possible via `refs/worktree/*` but would be destroyed with the worktree, so branch reachability is the right scoping; `--git-common-dir` resolves to the primary clone's `.git` dir; and `.cledger.json` is optional config this repo doesn't have, correcting my earlier \"tracked file\" phrasing. Flagged one real gap: the notes ref pushes wholesale, so abandoned-branch events still reach origin even though reachability hides them.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"c8094453047f0e5f8e3169519803050cbb5af009984b2352d836b00fd1c0cbbb","head":"47e117b2e20969745be383f2ab62bcc21a3dc5a5","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":823},"id":"ev1-60279fa9a2b1bce709bb8c311d41d3b235d61608f61f485adc08aeeb8511a09c","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-27T07:46:33.239Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.13.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Four answers.\n\n## 1. The sticky-note comparison — worth taking seriously, but the failure mode differs\n\nI should be straight with you on one thing first: **a per-worktree notes ref is technically possible.** Git has a `refs/worktree/*` namespace that is genuinely per-worktree — I just verified it: a ref created there from a worktree is invisible from the main checkout. So storing conversations per-worktree wasn't ruled out by git; it was a design choice.\n\nIt's the right one, though, and the concrete reason is lifecycle: **`refs/worktree/*` refs are destroyed with the worktree.** `git worktree remove` would take the conversations with it — precisely the loss the ledger exists to prevent. Worktrees are disposable scaffolding; branches ar